The quadrilateral ABB'A' is trapezoid with bases of length 8 and 12 with the diagonals being 18. We can draw both diagonals, \textit{BB'} and \textit{AA'} = 18. Also, the area can be calculated by splitting the trapezoid into 2 triangles where ABB' is a right triangle where we can solve for the area as well as A'BB'. Since the height of the triangles is the same, their areas are proportional to the bases. So, the ratio of the triangles area are 8:12 or 2:3. \hfill \textit{SECOND IDEA}
All we're left to do is split up the pentagon and essentially reduce it down to several triangles. \hfill \textit{THIRD IDEA}
The pentagon can be split into one rectangle and a trapezoid. The area of the rectangle is given by the multiplication of its lengths, 18 times 6. The trapezoid has the bases given of 6 and 12 and the height given as 12. \hfill \textit{FINAL IDEA}
\begin{tabular}{l}
Area of rectangle = 18 \times 6 = 108 \\
Area of trapezoid = \frac{12}{2}(6+12) = 108 \\
Total area = 204 \text{square inches}.
\end{tabular}
